  "worked_solution": "First, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+3}{4}=\\frac{11}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{11}{4}=\\frac{7\\times 11}{3\\times 4}=\\frac{77}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{77}{12}=6\\,\\text{R}5=6\\,\\frac{5}{12}\n$$",
